  ans: 2,
  exp: "The epiploic foramen (foramen of Winslow) is the communication between the greater sac and the lesser sac (omental bursa). Its boundaries are: Anteriorly—the free right margin of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament) containing the portal triad (portal vein, proper hepatic artery, and bile duct); Posteriorly—the inferior vena cava (IVC), right suprarenal gland, and T12 vertebra covered by peritoneum; Superiorly—the caudate process of the liver; Inferiorly—the first part of the duodenum and horizontal part of the hepatic artery. The Pringle maneuver occludes the portal vein and proper hepatic artery in the anterior wall. Persistent bleeding after a Pringle maneuver indicates injury to the retrohepatic IVC (the posterior boundary) or the hepatic veins draining directly into it.",
