  q: "A 17-year-old boy squeezes a pustular furuncle on his upper lip near the philtrum. Three days later, he develops high spiking fever, periorbital edema, chemosis, and lateral gaze palsy of his right eye. The infection spread retrogradely into the cavernous sinus through valveless venous communications. Which cranial nerve is the FIRST to be affected in cavernous sinus thrombosis due to its unique anatomical course directly through the center of the sinus?",
