  exp: "The superior laryngeal nerve (a branch of the vagus nerve) divides into a larger sensory internal laryngeal nerve and a smaller motor external laryngeal nerve. The external laryngeal nerve descends in close proximity to the superior thyroid artery before passing deep to the superior thyroid pole to innervate the cricothyroid muscle—the only tensor of the vocal cords (and the only intrinsic laryngeal muscle situated on the outside of the larynx). To prevent injuring the external laryngeal nerve during thyroidectomy, the superior thyroid artery is always ligated as close to the superior pole of the thyroid gland as possible (whereas the inferior thyroid artery is ligated far away from the gland to spare the recurrent laryngeal nerve). Paralysis of the cricothyroid muscle abolishes vocal fold tension, resulting in a monotone, husky voice and inability to produce high-pitched sounds.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Recurrent laryngeal nerve — Posterior cricoarytenoid muscle):</strong> The recurrent laryngeal nerve accompanies the inferior thyroid artery near the lower pole and supplies all intrinsic muscles of the larynx except the cricothyroid; its unilateral injury causes hoarseness and vocal cord paramedian paralysis.<br>• <strong>Option C (Internal laryngeal nerve — Thyroarytenoid muscle):</strong> The internal laryngeal nerve pierces the thyrohyoid membrane and is purely sensory to the laryngeal mucosa above the vocal folds (guarding against aspiration).<br>• <strong>Option D (Ansa cervicalis — Sternothyroid muscle):</strong> The ansa cervicalis innervates the infrahyoid strap muscles; its division during thyroidectomy causes no significant phonatory deficit."
