  exp: "Of the four primary muscles of mastication (masseter, temporalis, medial pterygoid, and lateral pterygoid—all derived from the first pharyngeal arch and innervated by the mandibular nerve, CN V3), the lateral pterygoid is the ONLY muscle that opens (depresses) the mandible, while the other three elevate (close) the jaw. The lateral pterygoid pulls the neck of the mandible and the articular disc of the TMJ anteriorly and medially onto the articular tubercle. When both lateral pterygoids contract together, the jaw opens and protrudes in the midline; when one is paralyzed (e.g., right CN V3 lesion), the intact contralateral (left) lateral pterygoid pushes the mandible across the midline toward the paralyzed (right) side.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Medial pterygoid):</strong> Although the medial pterygoid assists in protruding and side-to-side grinding movements, it is a powerful elevator (closer) of the mandible rather than a depressor.<br>• <strong>Option C (Masseter):</strong> The masseter elevates the mandible to occlude the teeth during biting and does not depress the jaw.<br>• <strong>Option D (Temporalis):</strong> The anterior vertical fibers of the temporalis elevate the mandible, while its posterior horizontal fibers retract the protruded mandible (it is the only retractor among the four muscles)."
