  exp: "The carotid sheath is a tubular condensation of deep cervical fascia receiving contributions from the investing, pretracheal, and prevertebral layers. Inside the sheath lie the common/internal carotid artery medially, the internal jugular vein laterally, and the vagus nerve (CN X) posteriorly between the two vessels. Embedded within (or lying directly on) the ANTERIOR wall of the carotid sheath is the ansa cervicalis (C1–C3 loop supplying the infrahyoid strap muscles). Lying directly POSTERIOR to the carotid sheath (outside the sheath, embedded within the prevertebral fascia on the longus colli and longus capitis muscles) is the cervical sympathetic trunk. Thus, retrocarotid dissection or local anesthetic infiltration behind the sheath can produce ipsilateral Horner's syndrome.",
