  ans: 1,
  exp: "In Brown-Séquard syndrome (spinal cord hemisection), three major pathways are disrupted at the level of the lesion (here, right T8): (1) The lateral corticospinal tract (which has already decussated in the lower medulla), resulting in IPSILATERAL (right) spastic upper motor neuron paralysis below T8; (2) The dorsal column (fasciculus gracilis, which ascends ipsilaterally to decussate in the medulla), resulting in IPSILATERAL (right) loss of conscious proprioception, vibration, and fine two-point discrimination below T8; and (3) The lateral spinothalamic tract (whose second-order axons cross in the anterior white commissure within 1–2 segments of entry via Lissauer's tract), resulting in CONTRALATERAL (left) loss of pain and temperature sensation starting 1–2 segments below the lesion.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Right-sided loss of pain and temperature...):</strong> Because spinothalamic fibers cross near their level of entry in the spinal cord while corticospinal and dorsal column fibers cross in the medulla, motor and dorsal column deficits are always ipsilateral and pain/temperature loss is contralateral.<br>• <strong>Option C (Bilateral loss of pain and temperature sensation with preserved motor function...):</strong> A cape-like bilateral loss of pain and temperature with preserved dorsal column sensation is characteristic of syringomyelia affecting the anterior white commissure, not cord hemisection.<br>• <strong>Option D (Left-sided spastic paralysis, loss of vibration, and loss of pain and temperature sensation):</strong> Hemiplegia and dorsal column loss cannot occur contralaterally to a spinal cord lesion because those tracts decussate superiorly in the medulla."
