  exp: "Weber's syndrome (superior alternating hemiplegia) is caused by infarction of the ventromedial midbrain due to occlusion of paramedian penetrating branches of the posterior cerebral artery (or basilar bifurcation). The lesion damages two key structures: (1) The exiting root fibers of the oculomotor nerve (CN III), causing ipsilateral oculomotor palsy (ptosis due to levator palpebrae superioris paralysis, 'down and out' gaze due to unopposed lateral rectus and superior oblique, and mydriasis due to loss of Edinger-Westphal parasympathetic fibers); and (2) The crus cerebri (basis pedunculi) containing the descending corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts, causing contralateral hemiplegia of the limbs and lower face.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Oculomotor nerve fibers and red nucleus):</strong> Involvement of CN III root fibers and the red nucleus (with cerebellothalamic fibers) in the midbrain tegmentum produces Benedikt's syndrome, characterized by ipsilateral CN III palsy plus contralateral involuntary flapping tremor/choreoathetosis.<br>• <strong>Option C (Trochlear nerve nucleus and medial lemniscus):</strong> The trochlear nerve exits dorsally from the caudal midbrain and does not cause ptosis, mydriasis, or a down-and-out pupil.<br>• <strong>Option D (Abducens nerve fibers and corticospinal tract):</strong> Damage to CN VI fibers and the corticospinal tract in the ventral pons causes Millard-Gubler (or Raymond) syndrome with medial strabismus rather than CN III palsy."
