  exp: "From the lateral geniculate body (LGB) of the thalamus, geniculocalcarine fibers (optic radiation) fan out into two main bundles on their way to the primary visual cortex (calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe): (1) The inferior fibers—which carry visual information from the contralateral SUPERIOR visual field quadrant (via the inferior retinal quadrants)—sweep anteriorly around the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle into the temporal lobe as Meyer's loop before turning posteriorly to terminate in the lingual gyrus (inferior bank of the calcarine sulcus). (2) The superior fibers (Baum's loop)—carrying contralateral inferior visual field information—travel directly posteriorly through the parietal lobe to the cuneus. Therefore, a lesion of the right temporal lobe interrupts right Meyer's loop, causing a left homonymous superior quadrantanopia ('pie in the sky').",
