  ans: 2,
  exp: "The clinical presentation of sudden, violent, wild flinging movements of the proximal muscles of one side of the body is hemiballismus. It is the classic hallmark of a focal lesion (usually a lacunar stroke) in the CONTRALATERAL subthalamic nucleus (of Luys) in the ventral diencephalon. In the indirect basal ganglia pathway, the subthalamic nucleus is the ONLY exclusively excitatory nucleus—its neurons release glutamate to excite the globus pallidus internus (GPi) and substantia nigra pars reticulata (SNr), which in turn inhibit the motor thalamus via GABA to suppress unwanted movements. When the right subthalamic nucleus is destroyed, excitation of the right GPi is lost, releasing the right motor thalamus from inhibition and causing hyperkinetic flinging movements on the contralateral (left) side.",
