  q: "A 2-week-old neonate presents with recurrent hypocalcemic tetany, a harsh systolic murmur, low-set ears, micrognathia, and a fish-shaped mouth. Chest radiograph reveals complete absence of the thymic shadow, and echocardiography shows persistent truncus arteriosus. Fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) confirms a 22q11.2 microdeletion (DiGeorge syndrome). Defective migration of cranial neural crest cells into which pharyngeal pouches accounts for both the thymic aplasia and the absence of parathyroid glands?",
  opts: [
    "First and second pharyngeal pouches",
    "Second and third pharyngeal pouches",
    "Third and fourth pharyngeal pouches",
    "Fourth pharyngeal pouch and ultimobranchial body only"
  ],
  ans: 2,
  exp: "In DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion syndrome / CATCH-22), failure of cranial neural crest cell migration causes defective development of the 3rd and 4th pharyngeal pouches (along with conotruncal cardiac defects and 1st/2nd arch facial anomalies). Embryologically, the 3rd pharyngeal pouch gives rise to the INFERIOR parathyroid glands (dorsal wing) and the thymus (ventral wing)—which descend together into the anterior mediastinum, pulling the 3rd pouch parathyroids below the 4th pouch parathyroids. The 4th pharyngeal pouch gives rise to the SUPERIOR parathyroid glands (dorsal wing) and merges with the ultimobranchial body (supplying parafollicular C-cells of the thyroid). Consequently, 3rd and 4th pouch dysgenesis results in thymic aplasia (T-cell immunodeficiency) and total parathyroid aplasia (hypocalcemic tetany).",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (First and second pharyngeal pouches):</strong> The 1st pouch forms the middle ear cavity and auditory (Eustachian) tube, while the 2nd pouch forms the epithelial crypts of the palatine tonsil.<br>• <strong>Option B (Second and third pharyngeal pouches):</strong> The 2nd pouch has no role in parathyroid or thymic organogenesis, and omission of the 4th pouch would leave the superior parathyroids intact.<br>• <strong>Option D (Fourth pharyngeal pouch and ultimobranchial body only):</strong> Isolated 4th pouch failure would spare the thymus and inferior parathyroid glands (which arise from the 3rd pouch)."
