  ans: 1,
  exp: "A horseshoe kidney forms when the inferior poles of the left and right metanephric kidneys fuse across the midline in the pelvis. As the fused kidney ascends from the pelvis toward the lumbar region during weeks 6–9, the connecting fibrous/parenchymal isthmus becomes mechanically trapped beneath the root of the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA), which arises from the anterior aspect of the abdominal aorta at the L3 vertebral level. Regarding renal histogenesis, the kidney has a dual origin from intermediate mesoderm: (1) The ureteric bud (an outgrowth of the mesonephric/Wolffian duct) gives rise to the entire collecting system—ureter, renal pelvis, major and minor calyces, and collecting ducts; (2) The metanephric blastema (metanephrogenic mass) forms the excretory functional nephron units—glomerular capsule (Bowman's capsule), proximal convoluted tubule, loop of Henle, and distal convoluted tubule.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Superior mesenteric artery; Distal convoluted tubule):</strong> The superior mesenteric artery arises higher (at L1), and the distal convoluted tubule derives from the metanephric blastema.<br>• <strong>Option C (Left renal vein; Loop of Henle and Bowman's capsule):</strong> The left renal vein traps the third part of the duodenum/left gonadal vein (Nutcracker syndrome), not the ascending horseshoe kidney, and the loop of Henle/Bowman's capsule arise from metanephric blastema.<br>• <strong>Option D (Celiac trunk; Proximal convoluted tubule):</strong> The celiac trunk is at T12 above the normal renal hilum, and the proximal convoluted tubule develops from the metanephric blastema."
