  ans: 0,
  exp: "Fibrocartilage—found characteristically in midline structures and high-tensile attachments such as intervertebral discs (annulus fibrosus), the pubic symphysis, the menisci of the knee joint, the articular disc of the TMJ/sternoclavicular joint, and the glenoid/acetabular labra—is histologically a hybrid between dense regular fibrous connective tissue and hyaline cartilage. Its two diagnostic hallmarks are: (1) It NEVER possesses a perichondrium, and (2) Its extracellular matrix contains thick acidophilic bundles of Type I collagen interspersed with regions of Type II collagen, with chondrocytes aligned in parallel linear rows ('Indian file' pattern) rather than spherical isogenous clusters.",
