  exp: "In the entire gastrointestinal tract, true submucosal glands (lying deep to the muscularis mucosae) are present in only TWO organs: the esophagus (esophageal submucosal glands proper) and the duodenum (Brünner's glands). Because Slide 2 exhibits intestinal villi with simple columnar epithelium and crypts of Lieberkühn (which are absent in the stratified squamous-lined esophagus), the presence of branched tubuloacinar mucous glands in the submucosa is pathognomonic for the duodenum. Brünner's glands secrete an alkaline (bicarbonate-rich) mucus containing urogastrone (human epidermal growth factor) to neutralize acidic gastric chyme entering from the stomach.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Lower third of the esophagus):</strong> Although the esophagus contains submucosal mucous glands, its mucosa is lined by non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium and completely lacks intestinal villi or crypts of Lieberkühn.<br>• <strong>Option B (Pyloric region of the stomach):</strong> The stomach has deep gastric pits and branched coiled tubular pyloric glands confined strictly to the lamina propria (mucosa), with no villi and no submucosal glands.<br>• <strong>Option D (Terminal ileum):</strong> The terminal ileum is characterized by large confluent aggregates of lymphoid follicles with germinal centers (Peyer's patches) extending into the submucosa on the antimesenteric border, not mucous glands."
